lties. Press escape key to return to main menu Arts & Humanities Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Law Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Nursing, Midwifery & Palliative Care Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Business Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Life Sciences & Medicine Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Natural, Mathematical & Engineering Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Dentistry, Oral & Craniofacial Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Social Sciences & Public Policy Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu About Navigation category. Contains links. Press enter key to expand. About Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu History Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Strategy Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Learning & Teaching Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Our People Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Work at King's Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Partnerships Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Governance, policies & procedures Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Professional & Support Services Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Diversity & Inclusion Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Financial information Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King’s Impact Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Venues Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Sustainability Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Students Staff Alumni King's College London Job id: 158386 . Salary: £80,125 - £85,125 per annum, including London Weighting Allowance . Posted: 08 October 2026 . Closing date: 21 October 2026 . Business unit: International, Engagement & Service . Department: Service & Impact . Contact details: Nicole Mennell . nicole.s.mennell@kcl.ac.uk Location: Strand Campus . Category: Professional & Support Services . Back to job search About us: King's commitment to serving society through academic excellence is central to its mission and Strategy 2030. Through its education, research, engagement and operations, the university seeks to create positive societal impact and generate lasting and meaningful value for people, communities and the planet. The IES Directorate at King’s brings together the expertise of teams engaging with communities and partners locally, national and globally. IES provides strategic leadership for the university’s engagement at home and abroad, while providing a home for a portfolio of innovative programmes that generate and measure impact and serve the communities we work with and for. IES is home to some of King’s most innovative and impact-driven programming including Sanctuary, the Afe Babalola Centre for Transnational Education, King’s Volunteering, One King’s Impact, among others, as well as our civic and place-based engagement in London, and across the world. About the role: The Director of Service & Impact is a senior leadership role within the International, Engagement & Service Directorate. The postholder provides cross-university strategic leadership for King's Service and Societal Impact agenda, ensuring that the university's commitment to serving society is embedded across institutional priorities, decision-making, partnerships and culture. A central responsibility of this fixed-term appointment is to lead the consultation, approval, launch and mobilisation of King's Service & Societal Impact Enabling Framework 2030. Working closely with senior leaders, faculties, professional services, students and external partners, the postholder will translate the framework into a clear implementation programme, governance structure and performance approach that supports delivery of King's strategic ambitions. The postholder leads the Service & Impact Unit, providing strategic direction and people leadership to the Sanctuary, Volunteering and One King's Impact teams, ensuring the successful delivery of existing programmes and the development of future priorities. This is a full time (35 hours per week), and you will be offered a fixed term contract until 14/01/28. About you: To be successful in this role, we are looking for candidates to have the following skills and experience: Essential criteria Significant experience of leading and implementing complex organisational strategies or transformation programmes, including consultation, governance approval and delivery. Substantial experience of line managing diverse teams and senior professionals, balancing competing priorities, managing performance and creating an inclusive, high-performing culture. Demonstrable ability to influence, engage and build consensus among senior stakeholders within complex matrix organisations. Experience of developing and implementing impact, evaluation and performance measurement frameworks, including outcomes-based approaches and theories of change. Proven success in developing and managing strategic partnerships with external organisations, communities, funders and senior stakeholders. Strong financial and resource management experience, including budget oversight, business planning and funding development. Excellent strategic, analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to operate effectively in complex and evolving environments. Knowledge and understanding of societal impact, civic engagement, social value or related agendas within higher education, public sector or charitable settings. Desirable criteria Experience of leading institution-wide consultation and change programmes within a higher education environment. Knowledge of UK higher education policy and performance frameworks, including REF, KEF and associated impact agendas Experience securing external funding, sponsorship or investment for impact-led initiatives. Understanding of social value frameworks and approaches to evidencing institutional impact. Downloading a copy of our Job Description Full details of the role and the skills, knowledge and experience required can be found in the Job Description document, provided at the bottom of the page. This document will provide information of what criteria will be assessed at each stage of the recruitment process. Further information: At King’s, we believe that the diversity of our community and a culture that is welcoming, open, inclusive and collaborative, are great strengths of the university. The Equality Act of 2010 protects the rights of our students and staff and provides a framework to fulfil our duties to eliminate unlawful discrimination, harassment and victimisation and in addition, to advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations between those who share a protected characteristic and those who do not. At times, this will include balancing rights and beliefs that can feel in tension. We are committed to free speech and to academic freedom, believing that our foundational purpose as a university, is to create spaces where a wide range of ideas, including ideas that are controversial, can be discussed and debated, and where members of our community can express lawful views without fear of intimidation, harassment or discrimination. When engaging in the robust exchange of ideas, we ask that our community is mindful of our Dignity at King’s guidance. We ask all candidates to submit a copy of their CV, and a supporting statement, detailing how they meet the essential criteria listed in the person specification section of the job description. If we receive a strong field of candidates, we may use the desirable criteria to choose our final shortlist, so please include your evidence against these where possible. Artificial intelligence (AI) is evolving rapidly, and we recognise its growing role in professional work. Applicants may use AI tools to support preparation of their application, for example to research the role or structure written responses. However, applications must reflect the applicant’s own work and experience. AI tools should not be used during interviews or assessment activities unless this has been agreed in advance as a reasonable adjustment. Further guidance on the use of AI in recruitment can be found here . We reserve the right to close adverts early due to the volume of applications we receive. While the closing date may change, all adverts will close at 23:59 to allow sufficient time for applications to be submitted on that day. We encourage you to apply at the earliest opportunity to avoid disappointment as once we have closed a vacancy you will be unable to submit your application. To find out how our managers will review your application, please take a look at our ‘How we Recruit’ pages. Apply now Apply now Connect with King’s College London Study Degree courses Footer navigation link Postgraduate taught Footer navigation link Postgraduate research Footer navigation link International Footer navigation link Summer schools Footer navigation link Student experience Footer navigation link Information for New students Footer navigation link Current students Footer navigation link Staff Footer navigation link 