nformation Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King’s Impact Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Venues Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Sustainability Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Students Staff Alumni Open search input Jobs at King’s College London Your career Navigation link How we recruit Navigation link Our benefits Navigation link Our culture Navigation link About King's Navigation link Faculty Marketing Officer Job id: 159845. Salary: £39,076 - £43,909 per annum including London Weighting Allowance. Posted: 05 October 2026. Closing date: 13 October 2026. Business unit: Social Science & Public Policy. Department: SSPP Faculty Office. Contact details: Tali Jones.  Tali.jones@kcl.ac.uk Location: Strand Campus. Category: Professional & Support Services. Back to job search About us King’s College London is one of the world’s leading universities. This role sits within the Faculty Marketing team for the Faculty of Social Science and Public Policy. The role operates in collaboration with the Global Student Recruitment and Admissions Directorate (GSRA). GSRA is responsible for marketing the faculty’s undergraduate and postgraduate courses. GSRA works closely with faculties and professional services teams to develop and deliver evidence-based recruitment, marketing and conversion strategies that support institutional and faculty objectives. About the role The Faculty Marketing Officer supports the delivery of student recruitment marketing activity for the Faculty of Social Science and Public Policy. Reporting to the Faculty Marketing Manager, the role contributes to the implementation of marketing strategies and campaigns that attract prospective students, support conversion and help achieve faculty recruitment targets. The postholder will develop a strong understanding of faculty priorities and audiences, using this knowledge to support the planning and delivery of effective marketing activity across a range of channels. Working collaboratively with colleagues across Global Student Recruitment and Admissions, as well as faculty stakeholders and specialist marketing teams, they will help ensure campaigns are delivered successfully and aligned with wider recruitment objectives. This is an excellent opportunity for a proactive marketing professional to play a key role in promoting a world-leading faculty. This is a full-time post (35 hours per week), and you will be offered a 12-month fixed-term contract About you To be successful in this role, we are looking for candidates to have the following skills and experience: Essential criteria Educated to degree level and/or experience in marketing, preferably within Higher Education with at least 2 years’ experience in a marketing or communications role Strong interpersonal skills and demonstrable ability to form and maintain excellent relationships with stakeholders at all levels. Ability to work independently in a fast-changing environment and meet challenging deadlines. Able to work on own initiative, able to identify and resolve issues promptly and/or escalating where required. Excellent IT skills to support campaign delivery e.g., MS Office suite, SharePoint, and MS Teams, PowerBI, presentation software and marketing tools. Experience and willingness in understanding and representing the student customer. You will need to have immediate availability to start the new role. Desirable criteria Experience of working in a marketing role in higher education Strong analytical skills with the ability to interpret data and make informed decisions. Strong written and verbal communication. Downloading a copy of our Job Description Full details of the role and the skills, knowledge and experience required can be found in the Job Description document, provided at the bottom of the page. This document will provide information of what criteria will be assessed at each stage of the recruitment process. Further information At King’s, we believe that the diversity of our community and a culture that is welcoming, open, inclusive and collaborative, are great strengths of the university. The Equality Act of 2010 protects the rights of our students and staff and provides a framework to fulfil our duties to eliminate unlawful discrimination, harassment and victimisation and in addition, to advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations between those who share a protected characteristic and those who do not. At times, this will include balancing rights and beliefs that can feel in tension. We are committed to free speech and to academic freedom, believing that our foundational purpose as a university, is to create spaces where a wide range of ideas, including ideas that are controversial, can be discussed and debated, and where members of our community can express lawful views without fear of intimidation, harassment or discrimination. When engaging in the robust exchange of ideas, we ask that our community is mindful of our Dignity at King’s guidance. We ask all candidates to submit a copy of their CV, and a supporting statement, detailing how they meet the essential criteria listed in the person specification section of the job description. If we receive a strong field of candidates, we may use the desirable criteria to choose our final shortlist, so please include your evidence against these where possible. Artificial intelligence (AI) is evolving rapidly, and we recognise its growing role in professional work. Applicants may use AI tools to support preparation of their application, for example to research the role or structure written responses. However, applications must reflect the applicant’s own work and experience. AI tools should not be used during interviews or assessment activities unless this has been agreed in advance as a reasonable adjustment. Further guidance on the use of AI in recruitment can be found here. We reserve the right to close adverts early due to the volume of applications we receive. While the closing date may change, all adverts will close at 23:59 to allow sufficient time for applications to be submitted on that day. We encourage you to apply at the earliest opportunity to avoid disappointment as once we have closed a vacancy you will be unable to submit your application. To find out how our managers will review your application, please take a look at our ‘ How we Recruit ’ pages. Apply now Apply now How we recruit At King’s we seek to recruit and retain skilled and engaged colleagues to deliver the competitive advantage. King’s benefits Our pay and benefits package attracts the best candidates from around the world. Creating meaningful impact Addressing urgent global challenges to shape a better world. Connect with King’s College London Study Degree courses Footer navigation link Postgraduate taught Footer navigation link Postgraduate research Footer navigation link International Footer navigation link Summer schools Footer navigation link Student experience Footer navigation link Information for New students Footer navigation link Current students Footer navigation link Staff Footer navigation link Alumni Footer navigation link Facilities Student services Footer navigation link Accommodation Footer navigation link Libraries & Collections Footer navigation link King’s IT Footer navigation link Academic Calendar Footer navigation link Discover King's News Centre Footer navigation link Events Footer navigation link Student's Union Footer navigation link Visit King's Footer navigation link Work at King's Footer navigation link King's community Footer navigation link Contact us +44 (0)20 7836 5454 King's College London Strand London WC2R 2LS United Kingdom Terms & conditions Footer navigation link Privacy policy Footer navigation link Modern slavery statement Footer navigation link Site help Footer navigation link A