ulties. Press escape key to return to main menu Arts & Humanities Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Law Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Nursing, Midwifery & Palliative Care Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Business Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Life Sciences & Medicine Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Natural, Mathematical & Engineering Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Dentistry, Oral & Craniofacial Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Social Sciences & Public Policy Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu About Navigation category. Contains links. Press enter key to expand. About Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu History Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Strategy Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Learning & Teaching Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Our People Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Work at King's Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Partnerships Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Governance, policies & procedures Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Professional & Support Services Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Diversity & Inclusion Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Financial information Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King’s Impact Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Venues Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Sustainability Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Students Staff Alumni King's College London Job id: 157065 . Salary: £45,031 - £52,514 per annum including London Weighting Allowance . Posted: 02 September 2026 . Closing date: 12 October 2026 . Business unit: Social Science & Public Policy . Department: Department of War Studies . Contact details: Dr John Stone . john.stone@kcl.ac.uk Location: Strand Campus . Category: Academic & Teaching . Back to job search About us: King's College London is one of the world's leading universities, renowned for the excellence and impact of its research, its outstanding student experience, and its commitment to addressing the most pressing challenges facing society. As a member of the Russell Group and a global institution located in the heart of London, King's brings together a diverse community of scholars, students and partners from around the world. This position is based in the Department of War Studies, part of the School of Security Studies within the Faculty of Social Science & Public Policy. Founded in 1962, War Studies is widely recognised as one of the world's leading centres for the study of war, conflict, strategy, security and international affairs. The Department's interdisciplinary research and teaching span strategic studies, international relations, military history, defence studies, conflict and security, and emerging challenges in warfare and global politics. The Department is home to a vibrant and internationally diverse academic community with strong links to policymakers, armed forces, international organisations and civil society. We are committed to research excellence, intellectual innovation and delivering an outstanding educational experience to students from a wide range of backgrounds. We are seeking an ambitious and collaborative scholar who will contribute to the Department's international reputation for excellence in research and teaching, while helping to shape the future of strategic studies and the understanding of contemporary conflict. About the role: The Department of War Studies seeks to appoint a Lecturer in War Studies with specialist expertise in Contemporary Security and Strategy to strengthen and expand its internationally recognised research and teaching in military strategy, warfare, and armed conflict. The successful candidate will contribute to the Department's intellectual leadership by advancing innovative research in contemporary security and strategy, securing external funding, supervising PhD students and delivering high-quality teaching across our undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. We welcome applications from scholars whose work examines contemporary strategic challenges and who are able to explain the dynamics of a wide range of contemporary wars using strategic theory. We are particularly interested in candidates whose expertise complements existing departmental strengths and enhances our understanding of areas such as transnational conflicts and organised crime, multi-domain operations, grey-zone and hybrid warfare,, or other developing dimensions and domains of strategic competition and conflict. The role offers an opportunity to contribute to a vibrant and collaborative academic community, engage with policymakers and practitioners, and help shape the future direction of research and education in one of the world's leading centres for the study of war, strategy, and security. This is a full-time post on an indefinite contract. About you: To be successful in this role, we are looking for candidates to have the following skills and experience: Essential criteria PhD awarded (or close to completion and submitted by the date of appointment) in War Studies, Security Studies, International Relations, Strategic Studies, Political Science, or a closely related discipline. Evidence of an emerging programme of high-quality research in military strategy, contemporary warfare, armed conflict, or a related field, with a clear trajectory towards internationally recognised outputs. Demonstrable record of research publication and dissemination appropriate to career stage, together with a credible strategy for future research development and impact. Ability to design and deliver high-quality, research-led teaching at undergraduate and/or postgraduate level, including the capacity to connect strategic theory with contemporary empirical cases. Ability to work collaboratively and make a positive contribution to the intellectual life, administration and wider academic community of the Department, School and University. Excellent written, verbal and presentation skills, with the ability to communicate complex ideas effectively to academic, student, policy and practitioner audiences. Desirable criteria Expertise in transnational conflicts and organised crime, multi-domain operations, grey-zone and hybrid warfare, or other developing dimensions and domains of strategic competition and conflict that strengthens and diversifies existing departmental expertise. Experience of securing or contributing to externally funded research projects. Ability to assess student learning effectively, provide constructive feedback, and contribute to an inclusive and supportive learning environment that promotes student success. Experience of supervising undergraduate or postgraduate research projects. Fellowship of Advance HE (or willingness to obtain it within an agreed timeframe). Downloading a copy of our Job Description Full details of the role and the skills, knowledge and experience required can be found in the Job Description document, provided at the bottom of the page. This document will provide information of what criteria will be assessed at each stage of the recruitment process. At King’s, we believe that the diversity of our community and a culture that is welcoming, open, inclusive and collaborative, are great strengths of the university. The Equality Act of 2010 protects the rights of our students and staff and provides a framework to fulfil our duties to eliminate unlawful discrimination, harassment and victimisation and in addition, to advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations between those who share a protected characteristic and those who do not. At times, this will include balancing rights and beliefs that can feel in tension. We are committed to free speech and to academic freedom, believing that our foundational purpose as a university, is to create spaces where a wide range of ideas, including ideas that are controversial, can be discussed and debated, and where members of our community can express lawful views without fear of intimidation, harassment or discrimination. When engaging in the robust exchange of ideas, we ask that our community is mindful of our Dignity at King’s guidance. We ask all candidates to submit a copy of their CV, and a supporting statement, detailing how they meet the essential criteria listed in the person specification section of the job description. If we receive a strong field of candidates, we may use the desirable criteria to choose our final shortlist, so please include your evidence against these where possible. Artificial intelligence (AI) is evolving rapidly, and we recognise its growing role in professional work. Applicants may use AI tools to support preparation of their application, for example to research the role or structure written responses. However, applications must reflect the applicant’s own work and experience. AI tools should not be used during interviews or assessment activities unless this has been agreed in advance as a reasonable adjustment. Further guidance on the use of AI in recruitment can be found here. We reserve the right to close adverts early due to the volume of applications we receive. While the closing date may change, all adverts will close at 23:59 to allow sufficient time for applications to be submitted on that day. We encourage you to apply at the earliest opportunity to avoid disappointment as once we have closed a vacancy you will be unable to submit your application. To find out how our managers will review your application, please take a look at our ‘How we Recruit’ pages. Apply now Apply now Connect with King’s College London Study Degree courses Footer navigation link Postgraduate taught Footer navigation link Postgraduate research Footer navigation link International Footer navigation link Summer schools Footer navigation link Student experience Footer navigation link Information for New students Footer navigation link Current students Footer navigation link Staff Footer navigation link Alumni Footer navigation link Facilities Student services Footer navigation link Accommodation Footer navigation link Libraries & Collections Footer navigation link King’s IT Footer navigation link Academic Calendar Footer navigation link Discover King's News Centre Footer navigation link Events Footer navigation link Student's Union Footer navigation link Visit King's Footer navigation link Work at King's Footer navigation link King's community Footer navigation link Contact us +44 (0)20 7836 5454 King's College London Strand London WC2R 2LS United Kingdom Terms & conditions Footer navigation link Privacy policy Footer navigation link Modern slavery statement Footer navigation link Site help Footer navigation link Accessibility Footer navigation link Manage cookies Footer navigation link"
