tion link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Financial information Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King’s Impact Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Venues Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Sustainability Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Students Staff Alumni King's College London Job id: 159787 . Salary: £39,076 - £43,909 per annum including London Weighting Allowance . Posted: 05 October 2026 . Closing date: 18 October 2026 . Business unit: Social Science & Public Policy . Department: Department of War Studies . Contact details: Alessio Patalano . Alessio.patalano@kcl.ac.uk Location: Strand Campus . Category: Research . Back to job search THIS VACANCY IS FOR INTERNAL APPLICANTS ONLY About us The Centre for Statecraft and National Security (CSNS) is the leading UK University-based research centre entirely dedicated to the study of contemporary statecraft and national security. Based at the Department of War Studies in King’s College London, CSNS brings depth and expertise to the making of national security policy today, through research-led professional development and direct engagement with policymakers. The centre draws upon the experience of the Centre for Grand Strategy and its pioneering programme on the Indo-Pacific to launch a new UK-India collaboration through the programme for the establishment of the first Regional Maritime Security Centre of Excellence (RMSCE) for the Indian Ocean. The initiative aims at shaping the debate and practices over maritime security in one of the world’s most dynamic regions through in-depth research, thought-leadership, professional development, and knowledge exchange. About the role The post holder will be responsible for the management and smooth running of the newly established Regional Maritime Security Centre of Excellence for the Indian Ocean (RMSCE), a research programme based within the Centre for Statecraft and National Security at the War Studies Department, King’s College London. The successful candidate will work in a diverse team including full-time academics and graduate students and will regularly interact with international practitioners of maritime security, and diplomats. The overall purpose of the role is twofold. First, the post holder will help shaping RMSCE’s research agenda around emerging technologies and their application to maritime domain awareness, undersea security, and maritime cooperation. Within this context, the post holder will identify new research, commission studies, and ensure research outputs can be use for knowledge transfer in policy environments. Second, the post holder will manage the externally funded and internal budgets for the programme, organise its events and communications, and provide support to RMSCE’s activities. This will include budgeting and planning for events, helping with external funding applications and reporting, and to advise and support the programme’s senior leadership. The role will work closely with the research and Professional Services communities within the School of Security Studies. The role holder will be responsible to the Programme’s Principal Investigator and School Research Managers. This is a full time post (37.5 hours per week), and you will be offered an a fixed term contract until March 2027 (Please remove if not a research-based role) Research staff at King’s are entitled to at least 10 days per year (pro-rata) for professional development. This entitlement, from the Concordat to Support the Career Development of Researchers, applies to Postdocs, Research Assistants, Research and Teaching Technicians, Teaching Fellows and AEP equivalent up to and including grade 7. Visit the Centre for Research Staff Development for more information. About you To be successful in this role, we are looking for candidates to have the following skills and experience: Essential criteria Educated to degree level or equivalent experience; Proficiency in relevant IT systems and software such as MS Office, WordPress, CMS etc.; Excellent written oral and communication skills, with an ability to engage effectively with a range of stakeholders; Administrative and professional services experience within HE/FE, particularly the coordination of projects across research and business functions; Experience of coordinating, planning, communicating, and delivering large-scale events to a high standard and on budget; Proven ability to manage and coordinate logistically complex projects and liaise effectively with a range of stakeholders; Ability to work under pressure, with a flexible approach, and a structured and highly organised approach to planning and delivery of projects; Some flexibility around normal working hours to fit into the activities of the centre, such as evening events; Desirable criteria Interest in, or passion for, the safe management and security of the oceans; Proven ability in managing budgets and the provision of budget monitoring information; Prior experience in, or exposure to, the study of maritime security and strategy. Friendly and professional manner; Ability to work well as part of a small team. Downloading a copy of our Job Description Full details of the role and the skills, knowledge and experience required can be found in the Job Description document, provided at the bottom of the page. This document will provide information of what criteria will be assessed at each stage of the recruitment process. Further information At King’s, we believe that the diversity of our community and a culture that is welcoming, open, inclusive and collaborative, are great strengths of the university. The Equality Act of 2010 protects the rights of our students and staff and provides a framework to fulfil our duties to eliminate unlawful discrimination, harassment and victimisation and in addition, to advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations between those who share a protected characteristic and those who do not. At times, this will include balancing rights and beliefs that can feel in tension. We are committed to free speech and to academic freedom, believing that our foundational purpose as a university, is to create spaces where a wide range of ideas, including ideas that are controversial, can be discussed and debated, and where members of our community can express lawful views without fear of intimidation, harassment or discrimination. When engaging in the robust exchange of ideas, we ask that our community is mindful of our Dignity at King’s guidance. We ask all candidates to submit a copy of their CV, and a supporting statement, detailing how they meet the essential criteria listed in the person specification section of the job description. If we receive a strong field of candidates, we may use the desirable criteria to choose our final shortlist, so please include your evidence against these where possible. Artificial intelligence (AI) is evolving rapidly, and we recognise its growing role in professional work. Applicants may use AI tools to support preparation of their application, for example to research the role or structure written responses. However, applications must reflect the applicant’s own work and experience. AI tools should not be used during interviews or assessment activities unless this has been agreed in advance as a reasonable adjustment. Further guidance on the use of AI in recruitment can be found here. We reserve the right to close adverts early due to the volume of applications we receive. While the closing date may change, all adverts will close at 23:59 to allow sufficient time for applications to be submitted on that day. We encourage you to apply at the earliest opportunity to avoid disappointment as once we have closed a vacancy you will be unable to submit your application. To find out how our managers will review your application, please take a look at our ‘ How we Recruit ’ pages. Apply now Apply now Connect with King’s College London Study Degree courses Footer navigation link Postgraduate taught Footer navigation link Postgraduate research Footer navigation link International Footer navigation link Summer schools Footer navigation link Student experience Footer navigation link Information for New students Footer navigation link Current students Footer navigation link Staff Footer navigation link Alumni Footer navigation link Facilities Student services Footer navigation link Accommodation Footer navigation link Libraries & Collections Footer navigation link King’s IT Footer navigation link Academic Calendar Footer navigation link Discover King's News Centre Footer navigation link Events Footer navigation link Student's Union Footer navigation link Visit King's Footer navigation link Work at King's Footer navigation link King's community Footer navigation link Contact us +44 (0)20 7836 5454 King's College London Strand London WC2R 2LS United Kingdom Terms & conditions Footer navigation link Privacy policy Footer navigation link Modern slavery statement Footer navigation link Site help Footer navigation link Accessibility Footer navigation link Manage cookies Footer navigation link"
