gory About. Press escape key to return to main menu Financial information Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King’s Impact Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Venues Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Sustainability Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Students Staff Alumni King's College London Job id: 160127 . Salary: £39,076 - £43,909 per annum, including London Weighting Allowance . Posted: 12 October 2026 . Closing date: 01 November 2026 . Business unit: Research Platforms . Department: Research Platforms Office . Contact details: Steven Lynham . Steven.lynham@kcl.ac.uk Location: Guy's Campus . Category: Professional & Support Services . Back to job search About us King’s Smart Trials Hub (STH) is a globally distinctive multi-omics centre, located at Guy’s Hospital Campus within Research Infrastructure, part of Research Management and Innovation Directorate at King’s College London. The Hub brings together state-of-the-art spatial and non-spatial ‘omics’ technologies to enable full molecular profiling of cells, tissues and liquid biopsies and leverages advanced, multi-modal technologies to investigate disease biology at high resolution, supporting the equitable development of personalised therapies. About the role We are seeking a Technical Specialist in metabolomics & lipidomics mass spectrometry to join the newly established Smart Trials Hub (STH) at King’s College London. The STH is a globally distinctive multi-omics centre within King’s Research Infrastructure department, based at the Guy’s Hospital campus. It leverages advanced, multi-modal technologies to investigate disease biology at high resolution, supporting the equitable development of personalised therapies. The role holder will be part of an exciting, highly collaborative and inclusive team and deliver cutting-edge mass spectrometry-based metabolomic and lipidomic research. The role will focus on sample preparation and extraction from complex biological sample types, encompassing experimental design, analytical method development for targeted and untargeted analytes, LC-MS platform operation of high-resolution Orbitrap Tribrid mass spectrometers and computational data analysis methods. Reporting directly to the Smart Trials Hub manager, you will be required to work in close collaboration with a multidisciplinary team to deliver a research output of the highest standard to academic researchers and commercial customers, contributing to a wide range of research programmes This is a full-time post (35 hours per week), and you will be offered an ​indefinite​ contract. This is an onsite, facility-based role and the post holder is expected to be on campus for the full working week. About you To be successful in this role, we are looking for candidates to have the following skills and experience: Essential criteria 1.      MSc qualified in chemistry, life sciences or relevant subject area, or equivalent experience gained in a research setting. 2.      Practical experience with Orbitrap mass spectrometry and liquid chromatography equipment, including method development, sample preparation and troubleshooting 3.      Demonstrable expertise in data analysis and interpretation of lipids and metabolites using Trace Finder, Lipid Search and Compound Discoverer 4.      Experience working to health and safety, quality and research integrity standards within a laboratory or research environment, with a strong commitment to accurate record keeping, data quality, reproducibility and attention to detail 5.      Ability to communicate technical and scientific information clearly in written reports, presentations and discussions to a range of audiences (technical and non-technical) 6.      Experience working effectively within multidisciplinary research teams, building positive and effective working relationships with colleagues, researchers and service users, and contributing to the successful delivery of shared objectives 7.      Demonstrated ability to work independently, manage own workload effectively, and prioritise competing demands across multiple projects to meet deadlines and deliver high-quality outcomes 8.      Experience providing training, guidance and technical support to colleagues or users in mass spectrometry and liquid chromatography techniques Desirable criteria 1.      Experience with automated sample preparation and robotic liquid handling 2.      Knowledge of advanced fragmentation methods (e.g. UVPD, ETD) 3.      Experience with large clinical cohort studies Downloading a copy of our Job Description Full details of the role and the skills, knowledge and experience required can be found in the Job Description document, provided at the bottom of the page. This document will provide information of what criteria will be assessed at each stage of the recruitment process. Further Information At King’s, we believe that the diversity of our community and a culture that is welcoming, open, inclusive and collaborative, are great strengths of the university. The Equality Act of 2010 protects the rights of our students and staff and provides a framework to fulfil our duties to eliminate unlawful discrimination, harassment and victimisation and in addition, to advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations between those who share a protected characteristic and those who do not. At times, this will include balancing rights and beliefs that can feel in tension. We are committed to free speech and to academic freedom, believing that our foundational purpose as a university, is to create spaces where a wide range of ideas, including ideas that are controversial, can be discussed and debated, and where members of our community can express lawful views without fear of intimidation, harassment or discrimination. When engaging in the robust exchange of ideas, we ask that our community is mindful of our Dignity at King’s guidance. We ask all candidates to submit a copy of their CV, and a supporting statement, detailing how they meet the essential criteria listed in the person specification section of the job description. If we receive a strong field of candidates, we may use the desirable criteria to choose our final shortlist, so please include your evidence against these where possible. We reserve the right to close adverts early due to the volume of applications we receive. While the closing date may change, all adverts will close at 23:59 to allow sufficient time for applications to be submitted on that day. We encourage you to apply at the earliest opportunity to avoid disappointment as once we have closed a vacancy you will be unable to submit your application. To find out how our managers will review your application, please take a look at our ‘ How we Recruit ’ pages. Apply now Apply now Connect with King’s College London Study Degree courses Footer navigation link Postgraduate taught Footer navigation link Postgraduate research Footer navigation link International Footer navigation link Summer schools Footer navigation link Student experience Footer navigation link Information for New students Footer navigation link Current students Footer navigation link Staff Footer navigation link Alumni Footer navigation link Facilities Student services Footer navigation link Accommodation Footer navigation link Libraries & Collections Footer navigation link King’s IT Footer navigation link Academic Calendar Footer navigation link Discover King's News Centre Footer navigation link Events Footer navigation link Student's Union Footer navigation link Visit King's Footer navigation link Work at King's Footer navigation link King's community Footer navigation link Contact us +44 (0)20 7836 5454 King's College London Strand London WC2R 2LS United Kingdom Terms & conditions Footer navigation link Privacy policy Footer navigation link Modern slavery statement Footer navigation link Site help Footer navigation link Accessibility Footer navigation link Manage cookies Footer navigation link"
